## 1. Add the New Keyword to the Scanner

First, add `TOKEN_CONST` to the TokenType enumeration
in scanner.h.

```c
typedef enum {
  // Existing token types...
  TOKEN_CONST,
  // Remaining token types...
} TokenType;
```

Next, update identifierType() in scanner.c so
that the scanner recognizes `const` as a keyword
instead of an ordinary identifier.

In the existing case for identifiers beginning
with 'c', distinguish `const` from `class`:

```c
case 'c':
  if (scanner.current - scanner.start > 1) {
    switch (scanner.start[1]) {
      case 'l':
        return checkKeyword(2, 3, "ass", TOKEN_CLASS);
      case 'o':
        return checkKeyword(2, 3, "nst", TOKEN_CONST);
    }
  }
  break;
```

The scanner can now return TOKEN_CONST when it
encounters the word `const`.

## 2. Track Whether a Local Variable Is Constant

In Chapter 22, the compiler uses the Local structure
to store information about each local variable.

I would add a boolean field named isConst:

```c
typedef struct {
  Token name;
  int depth;
  bool isConst;
} Local;
```

The new field indicates whether the variable
was declared using `const`.

Ordinary variables have isConst set to false,
while constant variables have it set to true.

## 3. Update addLocal()

Change addLocal() to accept an additional argument
indicating whether the new variable is constant.

```c
static void addLocal(Token name, bool isConst) {
  if (current->localCount == UINT8_COUNT) {
    error("Too many local variables in function.");
    return;
  }

  Local* local = &current->locals[current->localCount++];

  local->name = name;
  local->depth = -1;
  local->isConst = isConst;
}
```

The depth is initially -1 because Chapter 22 uses
that value to indicate that a local variable
has not finished being initialized.

The compiler later marks the variable as initialized.

## 4. Update Variable Declaration Handling

The compiler also needs to pass the new flag through
the variable declaration functions.

Update declareVariable() to accept isConst and
pass it to addLocal().

```c
static void declareVariable(bool isConst) {
  if (current->scopeDepth == 0) return;

  Token* name = &parser.previous;

  for (int i = current->localCount - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    Local* local = &current->locals[i];

    if (local->depth != -1 &&
        local->depth < current->scopeDepth) {
      break;
    }

    if (identifiersEqual(name, &local->name)) {
      error("Already a variable with this name in this scope.");
    }
  }

  addLocal(*name, isConst);
}
```

Then update parseVariable() to receive the same flag:

```c
static uint8_t parseVariable(const char* errorMessage,
                             bool isConst) {
  consume(TOKEN_IDENTIFIER, errorMessage);

  declareVariable(isConst);

  if (current->scopeDepth > 0) return 0;

  return identifierConstant(&parser.previous);
}
```

The existing varDeclaration() must now call:

```c
uint8_t global =
    parseVariable("Expect variable name.", false);
```

This preserves the normal behavior of `var`.

## 5. Add constDeclaration()

Create a new declaration function for constant
variables.

```c
static void constDeclaration() {
  uint8_t global =
      parseVariable("Expect constant name.", true);

  consume(TOKEN_EQUAL,
          "Constants must have an initializer.");

  expression();

  consume(TOKEN_SEMICOLON,
          "Expect ';' after constant declaration.");

  defineVariable(global);
}
```

Requiring an initializer ensures that the constant
has a value when its declaration is completed.

The existing defineVariable() function can be
used to finish initializing local variables.

## 6. Recognize const Declarations

Update declaration() in compiler.c.

```c
static void declaration() {
  if (match(TOKEN_CONST)) {
    constDeclaration();
  } else if (match(TOKEN_VAR)) {
    varDeclaration();
  } else {
    statement();
  }

  if (parser.panicMode) synchronize();
}
```

Also add TOKEN_CONST to the parser's synchronization
logic so error recovery recognizes it as the
beginning of a declaration.

The parse-rule table should include:

```c
[TOKEN_CONST] = {NULL, NULL, PREC_NONE},
```

## 7. Prevent Reassignment to Local Constants

The most important change is inside namedVariable().

Chapter 22 already uses resolveLocal() to find
a local variable's stack slot.

After finding a local, we can check whether that
variable is constant.

```c
static void namedVariable(Token name, bool canAssign) {
  uint8_t getOp, setOp;
  int arg = resolveLocal(current, &name);
  bool isConst = false;

  if (arg != -1) {
    getOp = OP_GET_LOCAL;
    setOp = OP_SET_LOCAL;

    isConst = current->locals[arg].isConst;
  } else {
    arg = identifierConstant(&name);
    getOp = OP_GET_GLOBAL;
    setOp = OP_SET_GLOBAL;
  }

  if (canAssign && match(TOKEN_EQUAL)) {
    if (isConst) {
      error("Can't assign to a constant variable.");
    }

    expression();
    emitBytes(setOp, (uint8_t)arg);
  } else {
    emitBytes(getOp, (uint8_t)arg);
  }
}
```

If the assignment targets a local variable declared
with `const`, the compiler reports an error.

The compiler may still emit bytecode while recovering
from the error, but the compilation fails and the
invalid program is not executed.

## 8. Supporting Global Constants

The local-variable change above does not fully
implement constant globals.

Chapter 21 explains that global variables in clox
are late-bound, so the compiler does not normally
keep a complete list of global declarations.

To support global `const` variables, I would add
a compiler-side table that records which global
names are constant.

Conceptually:

```text
globalConstants:
    "pi" -> true
    "limit" -> true
```

When the compiler encounters a global `const`
declaration, it would record the name.

When compiling an assignment to a global variable,
it would check this table and report an error if
the name represents a constant.

The table would need to preserve information across
REPL inputs and correctly handle global redeclarations.

A complete implementation must also account for
functions that reference constants declared later
in the script. A declaration prepass could collect
those names before normal compilation.

This would make the restriction consistent for
both local and global variables.
